    {"id":"q4","type":"mcq","prompt":"The 1986 Anti-Drug Abuse Act's 100:1 federal sentencing disparity applied to which two substances?","options":["Heroin and methadone","Cannabis and cocaine","Crack cocaine and powder cocaine","Alcohol and amphetamines"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Five grams of crack, associated in public discourse with poor urban Black communities, triggered the same mandatory minimum as five hundred grams of pharmacologically near-identical powder cocaine, associated with wealthier users — reduced to 18:1 only in 2010."},
    {"id":"q5","type":"open","prompt":"The lecture points out an irony in Karl Marx's 1843 description of religion as 'the opium of the people.' What is that irony, and how does the lecture use it to frame its overall argument?","rubric":"Marx used opium as a metaphor for a sedative that dulls the pain of oppression without curing its cause. Within a decade, opium became literally what he described as metaphor: British imperial policy used the actual drug to extract wealth from Qing China (via the silver/copper currency crisis it triggered), while showing no comparable restraint about its own population's opium consumption at home. The lecture uses this to frame its conclusion that drug regulation across history has tracked power and profit, not danger — the metaphor becoming literal state policy captures how thin the line is between drugs as social control and drugs as literal commerce."},
    {"id":"q6","type":"open","prompt":"Compare the marketing of OxyContin by Purdue Pharma in the 1990s to the British East India Company's Bengal opium monopoly in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. What structural features do the two cases share, according to the lecture?","rubric":"Both involve an economic elite (the Company/its shareholders and the British Treasury; the Sackler family and Purdue) manufacturing or controlling supply of an addictive drug and aggressively marketing or selling it (auctioning opium to country traders with plausible deniability; citing a misused 1980 NEJM letter to claim under 1% addiction risk). Both concentrated the resulting harm in a population with comparatively little power to resist — Qing China's peasantry via the silver-copper currency crisis; deindustrialized Appalachian and Rust Belt communities. Both saw the responsible elite extract enormous wealth (Bengal opium revenue; an estimated $11 billion extracted by the Sacklers) while facing, at most, financial or diplomatic consequences (a war fought to preserve access in one case; a bankruptcy settlement, later partly overturned by the Supreme Court in Harrington v. Purdue Pharma, in the other) rather than the criminal accountability an ordinary drug dealer would face."}
  ]$drugs_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'history/thematic-history/history-and-drugs', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
